@model Profile
@*
    The hero's animated background (ADR-0008, amended 2026-11-14): a looping video of the owner's
    own stack at work, with a few quiet technology logos over it. It is deliberately a different
    surface from the faint code-and-logo layer fixed behind the rest of the page (see
    _Layout.cshtml), so the hero reads as its own banner. Purely decorative, so it is hidden from
    assistive technology and never takes pointer events.

    The technologies are shared between the two layers so each logo appears once: this layer
    takes the first TechLogoData.HeroLogoCount of Profile.BackdropTags and the page layer takes
    the rest. A content test requires every tag to appear in the projects, roles or skills and
    to have a logo, so the background can never advertise a technology the page does not back
    up (ADR-0006).
*@
@using Portfolio.Web.Content
@{
    var heroTechs = Model.BackdropTags.Take(TechLogoData.HeroLogoCount).ToList();
}
<div class="code-backdrop" aria-hidden="true">
    @* Looping video, made for this site: a request travelling through the owner's own stack. The
       page script plays it only on wide screens, with motion allowed and data saving off; otherwise
       a still frame of the same scene stays (hero-poster.webp, set in the stylesheet so phones, which
       hide the video, never fetch it). There is no autoplay attribute and preload is "none", so no
       video bytes are fetched until the script decides. Files: wwwroot/media; how they are made:
       tools/hero-video/README.md. *@
    <video class="hero-video" muted loop playsinline preload="none" width="1280" height="720"
           disablepictureinpicture disableremoteplayback tabindex="-1">
        <source src="/media/hero.webm" type="video/webm" />
        <source src="/media/hero.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
    </video>
    <partial name="_TechLogos" model="heroTechs" view-data="@(new ViewDataDictionary(ViewData) { { "variant", "hero" } })" />
</div>
